<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Mustang</h1><p class="st-meta">1380 CE – 2008 CE (approx.) · capital Lo Manthang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The small Tibetan Buddhist kingdom of Lo in the high valleys of northern Nepal, whose walled capital Lo Manthang survives intact.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ame-pal">Ame Pal</a>, a Tibetan warrior, unified the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kali-gandaki-river">Kali Gandaki</a> valley around 1380 and founded the walled city of Lo Manthang as his capital. The kingdom of Lo controlled one of the main trade routes between the Tibetan plateau and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lo prospered from the trade in salt from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> and grain from the south, and its kings built great monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubchen-monastery">Thubchen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jampa-lhakhang">Jampa</a> in Lo Manthang, whose murals are among the finest in the Tibetan world. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakya-regime">Sakya</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> predominated. Lo paid tribute in turn to western Tibetan rulers, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-jumla">Kingdom of Jumla</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha</a> conquests in the late 18th century, Lo became a tributary of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a> while keeping its own king. The monarchy lost its status when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-nepal">Federal Democratic Republic of Nepal</a> abolished royal titles in 2008. The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigme-dorje-palbar-bista">Jigme Dorje Palbar Bista</a>, died in 2016.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-mustang.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Mustang" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sopoforic · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mustang.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1380 CE – 2008 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lo Manthang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ame Pal, Jigme Dorje Palbar Bista</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism (Sakya)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Loke (Tibetan)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Lo, Lo Manthang</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1694&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mustang" aria-label="Kingdom of Mustang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6869 1111 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Mustang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7189 1367L7142 1363M7189 1367L7239 1418"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7142 1363h0M7239 1418h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7189 1367h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7189 1367h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lo Manthang. <a href="/atlas?year=1694&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mustang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

